I'm a product manager
here at Google,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.140
working on the databases team.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.920
Joining me today are going
to be Doug and Shashank, who

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.070
are going to help me explain
how Cloud SQL for SQL Server

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.250
can be set up and
provide value to yourself

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.083
and your organization.

00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:24.000
And all the familiar
tools that, today, you're

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.020
probably using with
SQL server on prem

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.463
that you can also use
with our Cloud offering.

00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:29.880
We're going to
kick it off talking

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.890
about an overview of the
different capabilities

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.400
and features that Cloud SQL
for SQL Server provides,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.840
and some of the
considerations you

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.600
should have when
setting up the service.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.480
Then, I'm going to focus in
on some more complex topics

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.880
that we generally get a
lot of questions about,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.610
such as replicated
persistent disk

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.240
and how that plays into our
high availability strategy.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.217
Then, we're going to hand it
over to Doug and Shashank,

00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:54.800
who are going to
share with you a demo

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.920
about transactional replication
and how to set that up.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.640
Finally, it's going
to come back to me,

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.900
and we're going to talk
about the next steps of where

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.069
Cloud SQL for SQL
Server is going

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.920
and some considerations about
everybody's favorite topic--

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.920
licensing and pricing.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.980
OK.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.250
So if you're not familiar,
Cloud SQL for SQL server

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.060
is one of three database
engines that we offer as part

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.960
of the Cloud SQL portfolio.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.220
It was released to general
availability early this year,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.950
and provides a fully
managed solution

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.520
that will provide common
tasks and reduce overhead

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.850
such as upgrades, backups,
patching, and other key aspects

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.070
to managing SQL Server.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.920
Today, we have SQL Server 2017.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.790
Four additions
that are available,

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:47.070
such as Enterprise, Web,
Express and Standard.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.720
It's fully compatible
with the tools

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.480
that you're familiar with
like SQL Server Management

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.520
Studio and PowerShell.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.360
And we can automate a lot of
tasks via the APIs and GCloud

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.650
commands, as well as the console
UX you may be familiar with--

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.080
just as you would with
both PostgreSQL and MySQL

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.330
as part of Cloud SQL.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.570
We show this map every year.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.449
And every year, this
just keeps growing

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.940
in terms of the number of zones
and regions that are available.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.810
And the key takeaway
here is that Cloud SQL

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.910
is available in
all these regions,

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.830
and SQL Server is no different.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.870
So you're able to have it,
Cloud SQL for SQL Server,

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.885
in any of the regions
that you are located

00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:30.100
in that GCP is expanding to.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.250
And this map will just
continue to fill out

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.190
as GCP continues its growth.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.270
So what you see here is actually
the cloud SQL architecture

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.310
that's common to all of
our database engines.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:45.130
And I thought I'd take a
minute just kind of diving

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.100
a bit deeper so that everyone
can understand what's actually

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.210
going on under the hood.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.170
And so on the left-hand side,
what you can see here is,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:58.300
actually, each instance has
their Compute Engine VM--

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.670
just like you would in GCE--

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:04.825
that has a Cloud SQL image in a
container with both SQL Server

00:03:04.825 --> 00:03:08.260
and all of our management
agents running on top of it.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.100
This includes everything that we
use for monitoring, management,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.905
connectivity, and the like.

00:03:14.905 --> 00:03:16.280
Powering that, of
course, we have

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.340
our network layer, which is
common to all Google Cloud

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.490
products, as well as Persistent
Disk for storage layer.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.390
And so whenever we
go and actually do

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.880
a deployment of SQL
Server, what we're doing

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.660
is refreshing the container
on top of the virtual machine

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.460
and then firing up kind of
the underlying storage layer.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.380
You'll see, in a few
slides, I'm going

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.990
to talk a little bit about how
Persistent Disk is actually

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:43.940
used for our
replication solution

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.640
for high availability-- and
it's important to kind of tie

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.190
that back into the
concept that you see here.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.620
So because we have
this separation

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.980
of the virtual machine on the
Compute Engine and this storage

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:01.130
layer, we're able to more easily
move between virtual machines

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:05.090
and do things such as live
migrate across regions,

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:08.630
or in different machine types,
change the characteristics

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.540
or parameters of the
machines themselves--

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.540
increased storage as well--
without incurring disruption

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.550
to your instance.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.829
Now, on the right
side, this is actually

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:21.980
the control plane that's
powering all of Cloud SQL.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.200
And so we have our
metadata servers

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:29.150
that provide us the management
capabilities to roll out

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.980
broad changes across the fleet.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:35.300
And so when we talk about
things like common interfaces,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.080
or any kind of new
capabilities, that we

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.860
want to provide to all Cloud SQL
customers-- not just SQL Server

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.080
customers-- they're
actually deployed

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.840
on the control plane
themselves, such that they can

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.150
be accessed by all instances.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.020
So talking about what that
deployment looks like,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.610
and what that
creation looks like,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.145
from the customer perspective.

00:04:59.145 --> 00:05:00.520
I wanted to take
a minute talking

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.400
about provisioning a SQL
Server instance in Cloud SQL.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.390
And so you'll notice if
you've gone through this flow

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:11.920
that we have the same machine
types that you're able to do

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.715
again for Postgres or MySQL.

00:05:13.715 --> 00:05:15.340
But if you're familiar
with SQL Server,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.270
you'll know that it can take
advantage of the full breadth

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.540
and capability of the
resources that we allocate

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.250
to that machine site-- which,
today, is up to 96 virtual CPU

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:31.870
cores, 624 gigs of RAM
and a 30 terabyte SSD.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.530
One key difference here is
that SQL Server in Cloud SQL

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.210
only runs on SSD--

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.770
you don't have an HDD option.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.580
We found that there's
increased resiliency and less

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.765
issues by only providing
that one option.

00:05:45.765 --> 00:05:47.890
Now, you can initiate the
creation of this machine,

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:51.370
or a modification of an
existing instance, again,

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.060
via the console at UX,
GCloud commands or our API--

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.570
much as you would for any
other CloudSQL instance.

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.565
Again, the most
important thing here when

00:06:01.565 --> 00:06:03.940
you're going and creating that
instance to make it useful

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:05.523
is you want to seed
it with your data.

00:06:05.523 --> 00:06:07.570
And so there's a number
of different options

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.190
here, one of which
we're going to talk

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.480
about a little bit later--

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.270
to actually use
transactional replication

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.860
to set up Cloud
SQL as a subscriber

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.710
and have that kind
of active replication

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.320
to bring in the data
with minimal disruption

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.700
and minimal downtime.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.890
But of course, you can always
import into Cloud SQL as well.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.910
And so you can import
[INAUDIBLE] native files

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:32.200
and be able to see your data
that way as well, offline.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.240
Now, I mentioned it before,
but once your CloudSQL instance

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.490
is in operation,
you have a number

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.880
of different other options and
parameters that you can set.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.300
One of which that we've always
gotten a lot of good feedback

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.060
from customers on is
about the ability to auto

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:49.990
grow your storage.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.780
And so you don't
have to pre-provision

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.230
all of the storage that
you need from the get-go.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:57.710
Once you do that import--

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:01.990
let's say you're importing a
500 gig SQL Server instance, so

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.820
a number of databases that way--

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.420
you can set auto grow
on the disk and then not

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.820
worry about making sure you
have the correct allocation

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:14.890
for projected growth three,
six, nine months down the road.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.710
Additionally, you can set a
number of different options

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:19.670
just as you would with
SQL Server on prem.

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.940
So if you're familiar
with the command using

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.335
the stored procedure
for SP_Configure,

00:07:24.335 --> 00:07:26.710
you'll know that you can set
a lot of those same settings

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.200
in our console,
and via our APIs,

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.940
with the different options
that we allow under flags.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.590
That includes collation, which
is now available as well.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.720
For high availability,
we'll talk a little bit

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.800
and focus about how
that actually works.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.920
But it's important to
note that, in order

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:48.340
to really get the
most out of Cloud SQL

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.800
and really take advantage
of our 3 and 1/2

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.280
9 SLA you'll have to select
a regional availability

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:55.480
configuration.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.910
And then make sure that
you pay special attention

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.420
to maintenance windows and kind
of the appropriate time for us

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.030
to make any changes.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.770
CloudSQL will, of
course, do its best

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.550
to minimize any disruption--
have maintenance events happen

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:13.300
as quickly and as
infrequently as makes sense.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.230
But our first priority
is, of course,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.370
always making sure that
the service is both secure

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.700
and highly available.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.200
The final point I
wanted to touch on

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.720
was the automatic backups.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.440
We get a lot of questions around
how frequent customers can

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.470
take backups of their
instance, or DR purposes,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.610
or if they want to then
store them and restore them

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:38.400
into other instances--
either on other clouds

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.059
or backed on prem if they're
running a hybrid configuration.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:45.630
So, by default, you'll
have automatic backups

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.390
on a daily basis that you
can set a window when you

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.050
want them to actually occur.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.908
These are storage snapshots.

00:08:51.908 --> 00:08:53.700
So they're actually
snapshots of Persistent

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:57.300
Disk and should not incur a
performance hit onto SQL Server

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.090
itself because you're not using
the database engine to take

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:01.890
the backup, in this case.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:05.820
For more frequent backups,
you could, of course,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.960
automate a set of manual
backups and use the APIs,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.730
or use GCloud
commands, depending

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.340
how you want to
interact with it,

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.640
but then you need to manage
the retention of those backups

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.520
yourself.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.640
So with the automatic
backups, you

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.320
have a seven-day sliding window.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.630
With manual backups, we won't
drop any backup instances--

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.950
it's more of a manual retention
management experience.

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:33.120
But when you log
into the instance,

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.250
you'll actually see a
list of all of the backups

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:36.750
that you've taken
manual, and you'll

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.417
be able to manage them
from there as well.

00:09:39.417 --> 00:09:41.250
Of course, you could
always export the data.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.523
We allow a full [INAUDIBLE]
export as well--

00:09:44.523 --> 00:09:46.690
and you can leverage that
in conjunction, of course.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.370
But that does leverage the
SQL Server database engine

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.260
in that case.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:56.465
Next topic I wanted to touch
briefly on was analytics.

00:09:56.465 --> 00:09:58.090
Folks that are familiar
with SQL Server

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:02.120
are generally interested in also
using the auxiliary services,

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:04.360
such as SQL Server
Recording Studio, Analytics

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.150
Studio and
Integrations Services,

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:09.700
all together with SQL Server.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.950
And the best practice
that we found

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.740
is actually running
those services separately

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.020
in Google Compute Engine.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.830
From there, it's very simple
to connect to your CloudSQK SQL

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:25.180
Server instance via
VPC, and actually

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.760
have a reliable and
durable pipeline

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.630
that you can create for any data
analytics or transformations

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.010
that you want to do.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.600
Native tools will continue
to work as expected.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.840
So if you're using things
like Query Optimizer,

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.230
or you're using any
other Microsoft products,

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:44.890
you're more than welcome to
continue to use those, and just

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.480
connect to that
CloudSQL SQL instance

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.580
and it should
behave as expected.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.600
Now, if you want to use a
slightly different stack,

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.750
of course, Google Cloud
has a very robust analytics

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:56.840
ecosystem.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:00.348
And so you can use tools such as
Dataflow or Cloud Data Fusion,

00:11:00.348 --> 00:11:02.515
and just use the standard
connection JDBC connection

00:11:02.515 --> 00:11:04.900
string to go and
connect to the CloudSQL

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:09.670
for SQL Server instance, and the
specific database here you're

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.223
working with, and then set up
a data pipeline in that manner.

00:11:13.223 --> 00:11:14.890
And there's a number
of examples that we

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:16.820
have those in documentation.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.840
In that manner, you
can bring that data

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.080
into other destinations--

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:23.830
for example, BigQuery,
if that's something

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.825
that you're interested in
and familiar with using for--

00:11:25.825 --> 00:11:28.300
and those integrations will
continue to kind of deepen

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:30.000
and become more
robust over time.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.800
I'll talk a little bit
more about that at the end.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.840
The third topic that I wanted
to touch on-- which is, again,

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.860
something near and dear
to all of our hearts,

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:44.040
and better to talk about this
proactively than reactively

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.130
if something happens--

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:47.700
is security, of course.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:50.370
So Google Cloud
prides itself on being

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.230
a leader in the security space.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:58.680
And there's actually a number
of Cloud-wide platform features

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.810
and capabilities that
we take advantage

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:03.540
of in CloudSQL, both
to ensure consistency

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:05.850
around all of Google
Cloud Services

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.210
and also because it is kind
of a best-in-classs solution

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.460
that we want to take
advantage of as well.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:13.470
So Google Cloud, if
you're not familiar,

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.190
encrypts both data in
transit and data at rest.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:21.215
And so at CloudSQL, we
use that in lieu of TE,

00:12:21.215 --> 00:12:22.590
just to ensure
that we don't have

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:26.220
that double encryption that
could cause a performance hit.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:27.780
As a result of
this, though, we're

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.140
also able to take advantage
of customer-managed encryption

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.420
keys, if you're familiar
with that functionality,

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.440
as part of our key
management store.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.350
And so just as you
would set that up

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.360
for any other Google
Cloud Service,

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.330
you can keep using
that for CloudSQL--

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.910
it'll work just the same.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:47.190
In terms of connectivity,
you have three options.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.750
And really, I've
ranked them here

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:52.420
in order of recommendation
for security purposes.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.230
So number one is private IP.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:58.300
If you're connecting to other
resources in Google Cloud,

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:00.810
This is actually the
easiest and most secure way

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:04.590
to connect and access
your CloudSQL instance

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.020
in your SQL Server database.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:11.340
You can set that as part of your
VPC or peer-to-VPC networks,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:12.990
if you so choose.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:16.470
There's numerous step-by-step
guides and quick starts

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.590
in terms of how
to get this done,

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.390
and you'll get the best and
simplest experience that way.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:22.990
Of course, this isn't
always possible,

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:25.410
especially if you're coming
from a different environment,

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:27.150
or connecting from
another Cloud.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:31.140
And so, for those cases, you
may choose to use a public IP.

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.890
And of course, that
brings a new surface area

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:37.030
of security risks and exposure.

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:41.400
And so we provide the CloudSQL
proxy that I've linked to here.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.640
I definitely recommend using
the proxy in conjunction

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.890
with public IP
whenever possible,

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:50.070
because it will manager
your SSL connectivity

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.390
and settings as well.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.420
And it's also not
going to require

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.870
you to authorize any networks,
which is another attack

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.770
vector that we've seen increases
exposure to your CloudSQL

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.460
instance.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.210
Finally, if you do want to take
on the manual task of managing

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.160
all of those aspects,
you're more than

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:09.960
welcome to use public IP.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.840
Those are available--
you'll get an IPv4 address

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.090
that you can go and connect to.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.550
And you can connect from
anywhere in the world,

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.670
as long as you add your
authorized network as well.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:24.540
The final point here is in
terms of the login credentials.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.210
So today, if you use
SQL authentication

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.450
with SQL Server as
part of CloudSQL,

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:33.660
we actually provide
you a precooked user,

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:34.845
which is SQL Server--

00:14:34.845 --> 00:14:36.720
a bit different if you're
familiar with using

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.990
SA or Sysadmin in SQL Server--

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.400
and it is a subset
of those permissions.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:44.220
We do this because we want
to ensure the integrity

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.140
and security of the service.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.650
And so you'll see we have
a list of functionality

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.680
that's available on
our documentation site,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:57.180
in terms of what can and cannot
be done by the SQL Server user.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:59.520
But the most important
thing to note

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:03.260
is, besides what's not allowed,
in some of the TSQL commands,

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:05.370
such as the grant
command, you have

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:10.410
to use explicit syntax for
actually granting as customer

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:12.430
roles that we define.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:13.890
And so I definitely
encourage you

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:18.150
to get familiar with the
syntax and the capabilities

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.640
of this user as part
of our documentation

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.348
before you just dive right in.

00:15:22.348 --> 00:15:24.390
But by and large, you'll
see most of the commands

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.400
that you're familiar
with should just work.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:31.470
So as I alluded to
before, I'm just

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.210
going to take a quick
minute here talking

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:34.710
about replicating
Persistent Disk

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:36.870
and how that feeds
into high availability.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:41.220
So this picture on the
side here kind of serves

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:43.658
the best purpose in terms of
showing you the flow of what

00:15:43.658 --> 00:15:44.325
actually occurs.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:49.690
Our high availability
is a regional solution.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:52.050
So you have these two
zones in the same region.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.600
The zones are, of course,
separated, as all of our zones

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.800
are, and you have your primary
instance here in zone A.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.260
Now, that primary instance
is that compute instance I

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.210
was talking about
before, and that's

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.640
joined to the Persistent
Disk instance, which

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.590
is located in the same zone.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:14.370
Now, that Persistent
Disk is connected

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:18.370
and replicating to the zone
B Persistent Disk instance.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:23.160
And they're always kept
in sync by this agent.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:25.680
And so whenever you're
writing to one instance,

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.280
before the write-to-disk
actually succeeds,

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.350
it writes to both
Persistent Disk instances.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:37.520
Now, in front of all this, we
have our IP address and load

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:40.430
balancer that's actually
governing connectivity

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:41.470
to the instance.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:43.280
So in the case where
one of our agents

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:46.010
notices that there's
a heartbeat failure--

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:48.560
the primary instance
is unavailable--

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:51.950
what will actually occur is that
we will spin down that instance

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.590
and then switch over
into that secondary zone

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.660
where we've been writing
to Persistent Disk

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:57.860
this entire time.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.700
It will spin up a new VM--

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:01.880
a new container of SQL
Server, essentially--

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:06.020
and mount that second
Persistent Disk to it

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:07.560
and reassign that IP address.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.680
So from an external
perspective, there

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:11.000
should be minimal disruption.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.097
And actually no
connectivity change

00:17:13.097 --> 00:17:14.930
because you'll be using
that same IP address

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.819
in the connectivity path.

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:18.660
A couple important notes here.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.470
This is not using SQL
Server's native capabilities,

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:24.119
and so it's edition-agnostic.

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:27.109
You can do this with Web
Edition, if you so choose--

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:28.580
or even Express--

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.590
and have an HA
configuration, which

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.960
is sometimes helpful to keep
your development environments.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.820
Or if you want a
really right size

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.050
and go lean with your
SQLServer instances,

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:41.750
you're more than welcome to
and still have HA capabilities

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:44.330
with our SLA backing it.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:48.920
Finally, I just wanted to
mention that typical times

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:50.875
that we'll see here--

00:17:50.875 --> 00:17:52.500
we don't want too
many false positives.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:55.070
So the heartbeat can actually
take up to 60 seconds for us

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:56.903
to actually determine
if it's a true failure

00:17:56.903 --> 00:17:59.120
or a true unavailable event.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.610
At that point, we'll
initiate the switchover.

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:04.070
Generally speaking,
optimistically,

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.950
you'll see it under
another minute.

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:09.540
Sometimes it could take
as much as a couple

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:11.390
of minutes on top of
that initial detection.

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:14.210
So just a few minutes later,
and you'll have that failover

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:16.680
experience.

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.740
And finally, I just
wanted to call out,

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:21.080
before we switch
over into the demo,

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.970
that SQL Agent is
actually running

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:25.830
on all of your CloudSQL
for SQL Server instances.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.880
And so you're able to create SQL
Agent jobs just as you normally

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:29.780
would.

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:32.060
Moreover, all of
the SQL Agent logs

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:34.190
are written out to
our operation stack

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:36.598
with Stackdriver, if
you're familiar with that.

00:18:36.598 --> 00:18:38.390
So you can set up
monitoring and triggering

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:40.770
just as you would with
your on-Prem solution.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.700
Now let's go over to
Doug Mahugh and Shashank

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:47.495
to talk a little bit more about
how this is done in practice.

00:18:47.495 --> 00:18:48.870
DOUG MAHUGH: Hi,
I'm Doug Mahugh.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.180
And with my colleague
Shashank Agarwal,

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:53.670
we're going to show you how
to migrate an on-premises SQL

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.860
Server instance to CloudSQl.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.600
This slide shows
a typical scenario

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:59.620
where you have an
on-premises instance,

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:01.290
you have an application
connected to it,

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.570
and we want to migrate
the data to Cloud SQL

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.730
without shutting
down our application.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:09.300
So to do that, we're going
to use SQL Server's data

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.100
replication functionality.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:12.900
As Ori mentioned
earlier, SQL Agent

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:15.600
is enabled by default
in Cloud SQL instances.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.652
And in the demo,
Shashank is going

00:19:17.652 --> 00:19:19.860
to show you how to do this
with SQL Server Management

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.740
Studio, which you could
use any tool that you're

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.710
used to for database
administration in SQL Server.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:28.230
Cloud SQL for SQL Server
works with PowerShell,

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.050
Azure Data Studio-- whatever
you're accustomed to.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.940
So you set up the
distributor node

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.000
to coordinate the
transactions, moving data

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.220
from the publisher
to the subscriber.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.680
In our example, we'll use
the publisher instance

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.480
as the distributor node as well.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.360
But in production scenario, we'd
recommend that be a dedicated

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:47.510
GC instance in the middle.

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:51.090
Once you kick off the
migration, snapshot replication

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:52.200
happens first.

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.600
Now, this could take
hours, because this

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:57.630
is moving your entire database,
all the history and everything,

00:19:57.630 --> 00:19:59.040
over to Cloud SQL.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.620
However, your application
can continue running,

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.560
because those transactions
are being stored,

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.080
and those transactions
will be replicated next,

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.130
and you'll have an
eventually consistent Cloud

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:11.760
SQL for SQL Server
instance that includes

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:14.520
all of the transactions that
took place during the snapshot

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:15.730
replication.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:17.350
So this shows what's
happening there.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:19.740
We're now migrating the
individual transactions over

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:21.900
to SQL Server in the cloud.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:25.240
And the final step is to just
migrate your application over,

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:26.970
change your connection
string, point it

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:29.430
to Cloud SQL for SQL
Server, and now you're

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.800
running in the cloud with
all of your data migrated

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:33.540
and no transactions lost.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.670
So now Shashank is going
to show you, step-by-step,

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.190
exactly how to do that.

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.800
SHASHANK AGARWAL:
I have a source VM,

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.800
which is stored in the
SQL server database.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:47.120
In your scenario, it can
be an instance outside CCP.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.220
This will be your source.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:53.790
It will be migrated into
an instance of Cloud SQL.

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:59.070
I have [INAUDIBLE] SQL
2017 standard instance.

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:02.120
So let's get on with migration.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.830
Let me RDP into this.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:07.350
So as you can see
on this screen,

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.420
I have already RDP'd
into my machine.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:15.890
And I have restored a
[INAUDIBLE] database,

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.240
which is one of the
sample databases provided

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:20.040
by Microsoft.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:25.930
And I would be
using this database

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:30.490
to replicate [INAUDIBLE]
to CloudSQL instance.

00:21:30.490 --> 00:21:35.140
And I have connected to my
CloudSQL instance as well.

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:38.320
And as you can see, there are
other databases inside there.

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:43.870
So first step to do this
is to set up distribution.

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.110
For convenience purposes, I
am setting up distribution

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:52.530
on the same node where the
primary database lives.

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:56.700
And this can be a
different node as well.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:00.820
So once distribution
has been enabled,

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.760
I'll set up publication.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:08.460
I am telling SQL Server to
use transaction replication

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.970
for my [INAUDIBLE] database.

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:15.750
And I'm then selecting
what articles will

00:22:15.750 --> 00:22:18.120
be replicated to the target.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:21.180
So, essentially, I'm
selecting all the tables.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:25.940
You'll notice that
[INAUDIBLE] table is disabled,

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:29.675
and this is because it doesn't
have a primary [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.250
This is something to be
aware of-- that transaction

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:40.840
replication did not work
with non-primary databases.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:45.070
And next, It's asking me if
I want to filter anything--

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:46.560
I do not.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:51.100
I'm instructing it to create
a snapshot of the database

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:54.490
to initialize the subscriptions.

00:22:54.490 --> 00:23:07.730
And then, I'm providing the
credentials for this publisher

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:09.190
to connect to the distributor.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:17.320
Since this DCEVM has
not joined any domain,

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:22.606
I'm essentially supplying my
windows credentials for it

00:23:22.606 --> 00:23:23.272
to work.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:32.420
And then, lastly, I'll provide
the name of this publication.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:39.200
So once this publication
gets created,

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:44.850
we'll see this in the list.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:56.210
And next, we will
add this distributor

00:23:56.210 --> 00:24:00.080
as one of the owners
of the source database.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:06.350
So I have added distributed
argument as one of the owners.

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:09.680
And now we'll create
a subscription.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:16.410
So I'm giving it
SQL Server Cloud SQL

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:22.350
instance as my subscriber
to this publication.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.710
Right now, I do not have
any database in this.

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:30.630
So I am instructing it
to create a new delivery.

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:33.120
And as this will
be done, you will

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:35.640
see a new database
generated on the right,

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:36.630
on the left hand side.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:43.100
And this will be a blank
database at the moment.

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:48.440
But as soon as this
configuration is complete,

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:51.320
then it will start
replicating data

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:55.840
into this
newly-created database.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:00.550
And once I provide the
credentials, my source

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:08.530
and target systems,
CloudSQLServer

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:11.200
offers connectivity
using SQL Server login.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:26.420
So let me finish this.

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:35.670
Once I will finish this, they
will start replicating data.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:41.220
And we can click on this and
view synchronization status

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.440
to show what's going on.

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:48.600
Right now, it is saying
we are stopping the agent.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:53.300
And if I will go into
my [INAUDIBLE] database,

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:58.400
I can see, already, the data
scheme has been replicated.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:05.690
And if I were instructed to
select some [INAUDIBLE] rows

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:07.900
I'm already observing
that some [INAUDIBLE] rows

00:26:07.900 --> 00:26:17.750
are populated [INAUDIBLE] Now
let's see how it is doing.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:26.150
So that would conclude our demo.

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.250
Any further changes
in the source database

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:32.900
will be automatically
replicated into the target.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:34.150
DOUG MAHUGH: Thanks, Shashank.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:35.775
And now we're going
to go back to Ori--

00:26:35.775 --> 00:26:38.430
he's going to tell us a little
bit about pricing, licensing,

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:42.695
and what's coming next for
CloudSQL for SQL Server.

00:26:42.695 --> 00:26:44.570
ORI KASHI: Thanks so
much, Doug and Shashank.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:47.420
It was really great to see
transaction replication live,

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:51.518
and see how to use CloudSQL
for SQL Server in practice.

00:26:51.518 --> 00:26:53.060
So now I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:56.450
about everyone's favorite,
or least favorite, topic--

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:57.840
pricing and licensing.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.905
It's really important to kind
of demystify this and make

00:27:00.905 --> 00:27:03.530
it a bit more transparent-- and
that's something that we always

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.280
strive to do here at Google.

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:07.640
So breaking this down.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.770
CloudSQL for SQL
Server is really

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:11.630
made up of three
components when you

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.120
talk about how we do pricing.

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:16.400
So we've got our
instance price, which--

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.300
if you're familiar
with CloudSQL--

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:19.940
both for PostgreSQL and MySQL--

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:21.660
there should be
no surprises here.

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:25.070
So you've got that same
region-specific price that's

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:28.190
comprised of core and memory.

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:32.030
HA Instances are going to be
twice as much on the instance--

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:35.150
mainly because we maintain those
two instances to provide you

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:38.550
that failover so you have
that standby secondary.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:42.290
And if you have watched any
of the CloudSQL presentations

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:44.450
so far at Next, you'll
know that we've now

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.400
released committed
use discounts,

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:48.060
and so those are now
available as well.

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:50.870
So if you choose to prepay,
you do get a discount

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.850
both at the one-year and
three-year commitment

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:55.742
levels, which can be
fairly substantial.

00:27:55.742 --> 00:27:57.200
It's additionally
important to note

00:27:57.200 --> 00:27:59.930
that that commitment is
actually able to be used

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:01.550
across all CloudSQL products.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:06.170
And so you can go and
use your committed ML,

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:08.360
both for SQL Server and
let's say for PostgreSQL,

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.370
if you're interested
in having applications

00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:13.610
that leverage databases
of both types.

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:15.830
Now, the unique
part for SQL Server

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:17.690
is, of course, the licensing.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:19.550
As a partner reseller
for Microsoft,

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:23.600
we do offer the
SQL Server license

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:26.790
that we have to as part
of our CloudSQL offering.

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:29.510
Now, the nuances here
are very similar to what

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:30.710
you see in Compute Engine.

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:36.400
And so the license cost is
charged on a per-VCPU basis.

00:28:36.400 --> 00:28:38.630
There is a minimum of
four cores, however.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:41.610
And so even if you run
a smaller instance,

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:44.520
you will have just the
licensing component actually

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:46.520
be charged at four cores and up.

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.500
And this, of course,
varies by addition,

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:52.360
with Express actually having
no licensing cost in that case.

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:55.610
BYOL, unfortunately, is
not available for CloudSQL,

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:57.410
but it is available
for Compute Engine.

00:28:57.410 --> 00:28:59.810
So if that is something
that is a requirement

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:01.340
for your organization,
I definitely

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:04.460
encourage you to go
speak to any of the folks

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.692
over at Compute Engine, or
reach out to any account manager

00:29:07.692 --> 00:29:09.650
or Google support agent,
and they can guide you

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.270
through that process.

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:13.640
The final component
here is fairly standard

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:15.680
across all Google
Cloud products.

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:17.930
We have our storage
and networking price.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:20.210
The interesting parts
here for CloudSQL

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:22.820
are going to be how
we charge backups.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:24.950
Backups are charged a bit
different than capacity--

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:27.410
actually at a cheaper rate.

00:29:27.410 --> 00:29:31.320
But you'll see that kind of
that same HA fee difference

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:32.970
is baked in as well.

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:36.380
So you'll have kind of
capacity being charged twice,

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:37.710
but backups not.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:40.190
Now, finally, networking.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:43.520
You'll have IngreS being free
into your CloudSQL instance.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:45.020
But I would definitely
encourage you

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:46.910
to take a look at kind
of the Egress pricing

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:48.540
so that there are no surprises.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:50.990
Because based on how you
expect data and traffic

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:53.160
to leave your
Cloud SQL instance,

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.650
you may get charged
more or less.

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:57.500
So there's a bunch
of tools online--

00:29:57.500 --> 00:29:59.540
a bunch of calculators
and guides--

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:03.740
that will help you navigate and
reduce any complexity there.

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:06.680
So I want to take
just a brief moment

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:07.850
about what's coming next.

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:10.610
We're really excited
here on the CloudSQL team

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:12.548
about what's developing
in the service.

00:30:12.548 --> 00:30:14.840
So if you're not familiar,
there's a number of sessions

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:17.215
out there talking about the
Managed Service For Microsoft

00:30:17.215 --> 00:30:20.990
Active Directory that we
provide as part of Google Cloud.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:23.360
We showed this last year
at Next, in conjunction

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:24.710
with CloudSQL for SQL Server.

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.960
And that's something that
that integration is actually

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:28.770
coming very, very soon.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:30.510
So definitely keep
an eye out for that.

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:33.200
It'll be on, of course,
all the typical blog posts

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:36.590
and marketing channels
that we provide here.

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:38.030
Additionally,
there's going to be

00:30:38.030 --> 00:30:42.530
a number of new high
availability and DR options

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:45.120
that we're going to be
rolling out as well.

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:48.050
And so I definitely encourage
folks to keep an eye out

00:30:48.050 --> 00:30:51.410
for those in the coming period.

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:52.550
Migration improvements.

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:55.430
You've seen a little bit about
how you can manually set up

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:57.080
a transactional
replication, but we

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:00.590
will be providing an
additional and more

00:31:00.590 --> 00:31:03.650
streamlined functionality in
conjunction with our database

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:06.200
migration service that
we've also recently

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:08.070
rolled out as well.

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:10.437
And finally, a deeper
analytics integration.

00:31:10.437 --> 00:31:12.770
And so we talked a little bit
about how you can set this

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:17.410
up connecting to CloudSQL with
Dataflow and Cloud Data Fusion

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:18.710
to get to BigQuery.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:22.820
We're definitely focused around
investing in that workflow,

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:26.310
and how we can kind of
streamline that relationship.

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:29.510
So for folks that are familiar
with the Google Analytics

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:33.410
ecosystem, you should have a
much easier time moving forward

00:31:33.410 --> 00:31:36.980
using critical products like
SQL Server alongside those tools

00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:39.830
as well.

00:31:39.830 --> 00:31:42.340
Thanks so much for joining
us today and talking a bit

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:44.230
about CloudSQL for SQL Server.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:46.060
I definitely encourage
you to check out

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:48.730
